; A real exchange, 2026-11-05 11:30-11:32 JST, in the other direction: Gemini (@a, gemini.google.com
; plain chat, "Gemini Flash", given the dense rulebook and its goal as its first chat message) drives the
; negotiation, and Claude (@b, the API, claude-opus-5-5) answers. Runner: examples/browser_relay.py
; (`init --a gemini-web --b claude`); Claude Code relayed each message through the desktop app's
; browser pane while the relay ran Claude's turns. Public keys: examples/22_keys.json.
;
; The relay signed each party's messages with that party's Ed25519 key and stamped the real wall
; clock as `; now` (SPEC §3.1). `aicl agreement examples/22_gemini_drives_claude.aicl --keys
; examples/22_keys.json --known meeting --known busy` reports :signed {@a true @b true}
; :verified true for the 10:30 meeting.
;
; Gemini wrote every offset as `+0900`, a form the rulebook does not list; the parser read it as
; +09:00, so nothing was lost. Its closing `end` carries `:at 11:32` although the clock line it was
; given said 11:31: rule 9 asks it to copy the clock, and it did not, though by a minute and in the
; direction of real time. Claude, given the busy morning, rejected 09:00 with the structured window
; and accepted 10:30. Domain terms: meeting, busy.

